course: dictionary
lesson: 6751
title: The onset of trouble
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An onset is the start of something. Usually it means the start of something bad or unpleasant. «The onset of winter» means winter has just begun, and «the onset of a cold» means the cold has just started to make you feel poorly.
explain: Listen to how a word begins. In «cat», the onset is the first sound, «c», before the vowel. Phonics lessons split a word into its onset and its rime: c-at, str-ing. The rime is the vowel and everything after it.
explain: Stories use onset to mark when trouble starts. Toad, in The Wind in the Willows, boasts: «What carts I shall fling carelessly into the ditch in the wake of my magnificent onset!» His onset is the start of his wild attack, and the carts are about to be thrown into the ditch. Ask yourself: what has just begun?
fact: Onset means the start of something, often something bad or unpleasant.
fact: «The onset of winter» means winter has just started.
fact: In phonics, the onset is the consonant sound or sounds before the vowel: «c» in «cat», «str» in «string».
fact: The rime is the vowel sound and the sounds that follow it, so «cat» has the rime «at».
fact: Onset and rime help you read and spell new words: if you know «-at», you can read «bat», «mat» and «sat».
exercise: «The onset of winter brought the first frost.» What does onset mean here? | A type of cold drink | The end of winter | A warm summer day | * The start of winter
exercise: In the word «cat», what is the onset? | t | at | * c | ca
exercise: Toad says: «What carts I shall fling carelessly into the ditch in the wake of my magnificent onset!» What does onset mean in this sentence? | The end of a long journey | A quiet, peaceful picnic | * The start of Toad's wild attack
test: Which part of «string» is the onset? | s | ing | * str | tr
test: «The onset of a cold» means which of these? | A cold drink on a hot day | * The start of a cold | The cure for a cold
exam: Which split of «frog» gives the onset and the rime? | frog- | * fr-og | fro-g | f-rog
